# Solvent-Mediated Dimension Tuning of Semiconducting Oxide Nanostructures as Efficient Charge Extraction Thin Films for Perovskite Solar Cells with Efficiency Exceeding 16%

## abstract
The recent surge in efficiency and progress of organohalide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has been significant. The PSC performance is significantly influenced by nanostructuring as this varies the intrinsic optical, electrical, and electrochemical properties. Diverse TiO2 electron transport layers (ETLs) are solvothermally grown on the transparent conducting oxide substrate with different dimensionalities, 0D nanoparticles (TNP), 1D nanowires (TNW) to 2D nanosheets (TNS), by varying the organic solvent used. These layers feature enhanced optical transparency (≈2%-5% transmittance improvement compared to pristine fluorine doped tin oxide, FTO, glass) minimizing light absorption losses. PSCs constructed using 1D TNW or 2D TNS yield enhanced photovoltaic performance compared to the 0D TNP counterparts. This is a result of i) improved infiltration of the perovskite in the porous TNW or TNS network and ii) facilitated electron transport and charge extraction at the TNW/perovskite or TNS/perovskite interfaces, thus reduced interfacial recombination loss. Employing a bilayered ETL film consisting of a selfassembled TiO2 blocking layer and a subsequent TNW active layer, produces PSC devices with an efficiency exceeding 16%. This bilayered ETL film can simultaneously block the photogenerated holes and enhance electron extraction, therefore improving PSC performance.

The recent surge in efficiency and progress of organohalide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has been significant. The PSC performance is significantly influenced by nanostructuring as this varies the intrinsic optical, electrical, and electrochemical properties. Diverse $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ electron transport layers (ETLs) are solvothermally grown on the transparent conducting oxide substrate with different dimensionalities, 0D nanoparticles (TNP), 1D nanowires (TNW) to 2D nanosheets (TNS), by varying the organic solvent used. These layers feature enhanced optical transparency $1\approx2\%-5\%$ transmittance improvement compared to pristine fluorine doped tin oxide, FTO, glass) minimizing light absorption losses. PSCs constructed using 1D TNW or 2D TNS yield enhanced photovoltaic performance compared to the 0D TNP counterparts. This is a result of i) improved infiltration of the perovskite in the porous TNW or TNS network and ii) facilitated electron transport and charge extraction at the TNW/perovskite or TNS/perovskite interfaces, thus reduced interfacial recombination loss. Employing a bilayered ETL film consisting of a selfassembled $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ blocking layer and a subsequent TNW active layer, produces PSC devices with an efficiency exceeding $16\%$ . This bilayered ETL film can simultaneously block the photogenerated holes and enhance electron extraction, therefore improving PSC performance.
## 1. Introduction
Nanostructured materials have played a critical role in the recent advancement of many key energy-related technologies, such as solar cells, photocatalysis, lithium–ion batteries, supercapacitors, hydrogen-generating water splitting systems, gas sensors, and electrochromic devices. This is due to the advantages they offer: tuneable bandgap, controllable optical absorption, favorable charge transport, as well as other promising physical and chemical properties. 1 $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is an important n-type, wide bandgap semiconducting material that can be easily synthesized in a wide range of nanostructures, such as nanoparticles, nanowires, nanorods, nanotubes, and nanosheets. This makes it a wellstudied material for application in efficient energy conversion and storage devices. 1–4 Of particular interest are nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based solar cells, especially the dyesensitized solar cell (DSC) that has been widely studied and achieved a record power conversion efficiency (PCE) surpassing $13\%$ by using a photoanode consisting of $20\mathrm{nm}$ anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals sensitized with a zinc porphyrin dye. 5 Recently, well-ordered and well-aligned 1D or 2D nanostructures have been widely employed in place of 0D TNPs to facilitate electron transport and boost charge collection by providing a more direct electron pathway and thus effective collection of charge carriers generated throughout the device. 6–9  Purposely designed, oriented, oxide nanostructure-based DSC devices gave efficiencies approaching and even surpassing $10\%$ , 10,11  indicating the effectiveness of optimized light harvesting and charge collection characteristics for achieving high photovoltaic (PV) performance.
Deriving from DSCs, a novel organic–inorganic hybrid PV technology that uses organometal halide perovskites (e.g., $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3})$ as light absorbers, has recently emerged as a serious contender for alternative low-cost solution-processed solar energy conversion. The direct bandgap, high absorption coefficient, long exciton diffusion lengths, and very high charge carrier mobility of the perovskite make the PSCs highly efficient, cost-effective, and full of potential. 12–16  Significant performance enhancements have been achieved in a wide range of device designs: using a variety of electron transport layers (ETLs), 17,18  hole-transporting material (HTM) layers, 19,20  and perovskite compositions, 21–24  with or without compact and/or mesoporous oxide supports, 25–29  with regular 21  or inverted device structure. 30  To date, both optimized mesoscopic and planar PSCs have pushed the average PCE up to $\approx15\%-16\%$ . 19,21,25,27,31–33  For the regular configuration that characterizes the oxide-containing ETLs, interfacial modification for improved charge separation and carrier (electron and hole) extraction at the electrodes (anode and cathode) is a fundamental requirement for achieving high-performance PSCs. 34
Most high efficiency devices have used a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin film as the ETL, which is normally deposited through aerosol spray pyrolysis 35  or spin coating. 36  This generally forms either a thin $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystalline blocking layer or mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticle layer, which serves as an electron selective contact for the photogenerated electrons, and prevents direct contact between the transparent conducting oxide (TCO) and HTM. Similar to DSCs, facilitated light harvesting (improved optical absorption and light utilization), fast electron transport, minimal charge recombination losses, and good pore filling of the active layer with the light absorber are the four key determinants for achieving high efficiency in PSCs based on the sensitizedconcept. Obviously, the ETL/perovskite interface affects the optical and photoelectric properties; hence nanoscale control of the interfacial materials will highly influence the cell performance. 37  Solvothermal processes have been used to fabricate various nanostructures and are a preferred synthesis method because of the ability to control the structure, convenience, as well as compatibility with large-scale fabrication. Structures such as 1D $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods, 38,39  nanocones, 40  nanofibers, 41 and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanorods, 42  which can provide a direct channel for efficient electron transport and open porous networks for better pore-filling of perovskite, were solvothermally prepared and then constructed into PSC devices, achieving PCEs up to ${\approx}12\%$ . However, such 1D oxide nanostructure-based devices have achieved comparatively lower PCEs due to the limited photovoltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ and fill factor $(F F)$ obtained when compared with 0D NP-based devices, which suggests there is further potential for ordered and oriented nanostructures in PSCs.
The standard FTO/ETL/perovskite/HTM/Au PSCs are model systems for further exploring and enhancing our understanding of the effect of precise morphological control on properties that correlate to cell performance. Therefore, this model will be used to study intricate $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures fabricated with different nanoscale dimensions and properties on TCO substrates for PSC application.
Herein, facile one-step, green solvothermal routes have been used to grow a family of defined $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructured thin films directly on FTO glass: randomly packed 0D TNP, anisotropically oriented 1D TNW and epitaxially ordered 2D TNS. The solvothermal liquor contains aqueous potassium titanium oxide oxalate dihydrate (PTO), and an organic solvent, with different organic solvents being the key for controlling the hydrolysis and nucleation of titania, leading to the formation of the differentiated structures. To the best of our knowledge, growth of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films directly on TCO substrates through a facile solvothermal route has not been achieved, let alone producing a family of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructured thin films in such a cost-effective solvothermal process with great simplicity and high reproducibility. These three types of nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films were applied as ETLs in PSCs to maximize light harvesting within the device due to their good optical transparency, which allows for enhanced light absorption and utilization. The use of 1D or 2D nanostructures (nanowires and nanosheets) facilitates the charge extraction at the ETL/perovskite interfaces due to the improved infiltration of the light absorber and increased interfacial contact. Additionally, charge transport is improved due to the direct electron pathway as a result of the formation of 1D/2D electron transporting networks, compared with 0D TNPbased cells. This gives improved solar cell performance (PCEs of $14.02\%$ for TNW and $13.09\%$ for TNS compared to $11.61\%$ for TNP). A PCE exceeding $16\%$ (at one sun) was achieved for PSCs employing a self-assembled $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer (TBL) and a subsequent TNW active layer obtained by programming the temperature during the solvothermal process. This PCE of $16\%$ is the highest reported for NW-based solar cells. High photocurrent, photovoltage, and fill factor $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}=22.60\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1007~\mathrm{{mV}},$ and $F F=0.71$ ) were achieved primarily due to the optimized light harvesting and enhanced charge collection efficiency.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The solvothermal growth technique has been shown to be advantageous for the fabrication of high-quality nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers on TCO substrates with controlled crystal phase and morphological, optical, and electrical properties. 43,44  Additionally, the excellent electrical contact between the solvothermally grown layers and the substrate is beneficial for charge collection from the active layer to the front electrode support. Here, the solvothermal growth of nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films on bare FTO glass was carried out by using a mixed solution of aqueous PTO and an organic solvent (see the Experimental Section for details) at $190~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\textup{h}$ . Nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films that uniformly cover the FTO substrate varied in morphology depending on the organic solvent used. A series of nine solvents was employed: ethanol (EA), ethylene glycol (EG), diethylene glycol (DEG), triethylene glycol (TEG), polyethylene glycol 400 (PEG400), diethylene glycol monoethyl ether (DEGME), diethylene glycol diethyl ether (DEGDE), iso-propanol (IPA), and benzyl alcohol (BA).
The surface morphology of the pristine FTO (Figure S1, Supporting Information) consists of submicrometer sized FTO crystals with a pyramid shape. The solvothermally grown $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films follow the profile of the FTO crystals (Figure 1), indicating controlled growth of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films and the thin film characteristic of the samples. The surface morphologies of the solvothermally grown $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films can be classified into three categories: thin randomly packed 0D TNP (Figure 1a) were obtained using PEG400 (Figure 1d), EG, and DEGME as organic solvents (Figure S2, Supporting Information); oriented 2D TNS (Figure 1b) with random and epitaxial inplane orientation over the substrate surface for TEG (Figure 1e), DEG, and DEGDE solvents (Figure S3, Supporting Information); and 1D anisotropically oriented TNW (Figure 1c) for the solvents IPA (Figure 1f), EA and BA (Figure S4, Supporting Information). These geometric features for the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures are qualitatively derived from the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) observations. The small amount of PTO $(3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~})$ used would immediately dissolve in Milli-Q water. The role of the different organic solvents is to slow the hydrolysis of the $\mathrm{Ti}$ precursor and to coordinate to specific facets of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ crystal, leading to the formation of various $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures on the FTO glass. 45–47  A study of the mechanism for growth of these various $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures under varying solvent conditions is ongoing.
The morphological parameters of the investigated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films are shown in Table S1 in the Supporting Information as measured from high magnification SEM images, Figures S2–S4 in the Supporting Information. The thickness of the films was within $30{-}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ and was dependent on the film structure: The TNP film thickness was within $30{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ ; the TNS film thickness was $70{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; and the TNW film thickness was $50{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ . Due to the epitaxial growth, the oriented 1D TNW and 2D TNS films were thicker compared with the TNP films. The TNP films were composed of crystallites that ranged in size from 10 to $30~\mathrm{nm}$ . Whereas the TNW films consisted of wires that varied in length and density, with a diameter of $5{-}15~\mathrm{nm}$ and were packed to give an open structure with a high degree of porosity. For the TNS films the structures varied from thornlike sheets, leaf-like sheets to inverted-hollow-cone sheets, with the sheets having a width of $10{-}70~\mathrm{nm}$ and a length of $20{-}80\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Both the 1D TNW films and 2D TNS films have space between adjacent wires/sheets, which would allow perovskite infiltration. 39,48
The crystal phase of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures was analyzed using X-ray diffraction (XRD), Figure S5 in the Supporting Information. Two diffraction peaks located at $25.4^{\circ}$ and $48.1^{\circ}$ can be observed in the samples, which can be assigned to the (101) and (200) planes of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS 21-1272). The crystallinity of the samples varied with change in the solvent (Figure S5a–c, Supporting Information). XRD patterns of three representative samples, TNP–PEG400, TNS–TEG, and TNW– IPA, are plotted in Figure S5d in the Supporting Information for comparison. Both the 1D TNW and 2D TNS films showed pronounced peak intensities owing to their larger overall size as compared with the 0D TNP films, indicating the improved crystallinity of the former. Additionally, the enhanced (200) peaks indicate that the TNW or TNS are highly oriented along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis [001] direction, due to the formation of elongated nanowire or nanosheet structures. The various $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures in this work are possibly formed by the self-assembly of anatase nuclei in a preferentially oriented fashion directed by the solvent. An in-depth understanding of this oriented growth could guide construction of more subtle and refined nanostructures.
The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films exhibited comparable or improved optical transmittance compared to the bare FTO glass (Figure S6, Supporting Information). These optical properties make the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructured thin films excellent candidates for application in thin film optoelectronic devices, such as organic–inorganic PSCs. This is because the amount of light transmitted to the perovskite layer is not hindered, but could be slightly enhanced within the solar cell device due to the presence of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film.
To assess the applicability of the resulting $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films in PSCs, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ -based solar cells were fabricated using the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures as ETLs without precoating a dense blocking layer on the bare FTO glass electrode. The main device configuration was $\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}$ nanostructure/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ /spiro-MeOTAD/Au, where the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ was deposited onto the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures by a gas-assisted one-step spin-coating process. 33  The fabrication process is described in detail in the experimental section. The photovoltaic performance of these cells was measured under simulated AM
1.5G illumination at an intensity of $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ see photovoltaic parameters (short-circuit photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , open-circuit photovoltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , fill factor $(F F)$ , and power conversion efficiency (PCE)) with standard deviation summarized in Table S2 in the Supporting Information. Solar cells utilizing only bare FTO glass exhibited a poor PCE of $2.68\%$ , mainly because of shunting arising from direct contact between perovskite and FTO, which leads to charge recombination. 49  In contrast, the solar cells employing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructured thin films as the ETLs yielded significantly enhanced PCEs, from ${\approx}9\%$ to $14\%$ . This improvement suggests that the FTO glass has been sufficiently covered by $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to minimize perovskite/ FTO contact and thus suppress charge recombination, and that these nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films can efficiently extract electrons. Typically, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the TNP-based cells are higher than those of the TNS- and TNW-based cells. However, the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ of TNS- and TNW-based devices are higher than those of the TNP-based devices, and thus better PCEs can be achieved for the PSCs constructed using 1D TNW or 2D TNS structures. The reason for these differences will be explained below. Overall, the nanowires or nanosheets are better ETLs in the thin film PSCs, yielding higher device performance than the 0D nanoparticle-based devices.
To understand the intrinsic optical and electrical contribution of the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures in relation to the cell performance, three representative cells with comparably highperformance within their own categories were chosen for further studies: TNP–PEG400, TNS–TEG, and TNW–IPA. As Ar gas is blown toward the substrate during the gas-assisted spin coating of the perovskite, 33  this can improve diffusion of the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ DMF (N,N-dimethylformamide) solution into the pores of the nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films and facilitates evaporation of the DMF solvent. Top view SEM images of the TNP– PEG400, TNS–TEG, TNW–IPA thin films (Figure 2a–c), and their corresponding $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ -deposited films (Figure 2d–f) are shown. Cross-sectional SEM images and sketches of TNP– $\mathrm{PEG400/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ TNS–TEG/ $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and TNW– $\mathrm{IPA}/\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ can be seen in Figure S7 in the Supporting Information. The gas-assisted spin-coating procedure leads to infiltration of perovskite into the porous nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold (bottom layer). Surface coverage on top of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer is also achieved, with the formation of a highly uniform perovskite capping layer (top layer) with relatively flat grain morphology, which is in agreement with other literature observations. 25,26  Such a bilayer structure is effective for absorbing incident light and collecting photogenerated charge. 25  Also, the good surface coverage, microscale grain size and uniform grain structure of the perovskite films are well suited to highperformance PSCs.
Being flat and well-crystallized makes the perovskite easier to be covered with the subsequent HTM layer. The film thickness of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /perovskite nanocomposite and individual perovskite capping layer, as well as in-plane grain size distribution of the perovskite crystals of the investigated $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructure/perovskite electrodes are summarized in Table S3 in the Supporting Information. The crystal size within the perovskite capping layer is influenced by the morphology of the underlying nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films. The flatter TNP coating produced the largest perovskite crystal size, in the range of $\approx160{-}530\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while the rougher TNW thin film yielded the smallest perovskite grain size (in the range of ${\approx}80{\mathrm{-}}330\ \mathrm{nm}$ ). The cross-sectional SEM images (Figure S7, Supporting Information) indicate that all three films exhibited a bilayer architecture comprising both mesoscopic $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ /perovskite nanocomposite layer) and planar structures (pure perovskite capping layer with well-defined grains across the film thickness). The thickness of the perovskite capping layers decreased slightly with increasing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film thickness.
A perovskite crystal growth mechanism was proposed based on the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures, as illustrated in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ . Deposition of the perovskite solution on the mesoporous scaffold results in nucleation within voids of the titania thin films and on top of the titania thin films simultaneously, possibly with different nuclei density. For the TNP scaffold, large crystal growth above the film occurs because perovskite growth within the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film is minimal, leaving more perovskite for crystal growth on top of the dense TNP thin film. In contrast, the more open and porous frameworks of the TNS and TNW structures are accessible to the perovskite precursor solution resulting in the formation of more nuclei on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffolds, hence producing perovskite coverage with reduced grain size in the layer above the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film. Here, further crystal growth is inhibited since nucleation and growth has also occurred within the titania scaffold. 26
Figure 3a shows the optical transmittance spectra of the pristine TNP, TNS, and TNW thin films on FTO glass. The three films exhibited improved optical transmittance when compared with FTO glass (Figure 3b), indicating good antireflective properties for enhanced light harvesting. 50  On the other hand, the light trapping capability of the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructured thin films were evaluated using total and diffuse transmittance and the corresponding haze factor, Figure S8 in the Supporting Information. There is an increased diffuse transmittance in the TNW and TNS thin films compared with the TNP film. Both the TNW and TNS structures had a larger characteristic size of the titania that can serve as an effective scattering element in solar cells. 51  Further evidence can be seen in the haze factor, which is plotted as a function of wavelength from 400 to $800~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S8c, Supporting Information). The haze factor increases in the order of $\mathrm{TNP}<\mathrm{TNS}<\mathrm{TNW}.$ This is an indicator that the scattering of the transmitted light can be increased over the visible range and thus become trapped within the porous nanowire or nanosheet networks. This would effectively multiply the optical path and increase the optical density within the TNW and TNS photoelectrodes. Because of the increase in incident light being transmitted through the FTO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films to reach the perovskite photon absorbing layer, along with an increased light trapping and light interaction at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interfaces, the overall LHE can be improved by employing these nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films as ETLs. All the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures/perovskite films show excellent LHE, above $80\%$ in the wavelength region from 300 to $750\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information). This can be partially attributed to the higher perovskite loading due to the nanostructured oxide scaffold and efficient light trapping and utilization from the bilayered light harvesting films $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\right.$ perovskite nanocomposite layer and perovskite capping layer).
The LHE was higher in the $\Gamma\mathrm{NP/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ film across the whole absorption spectrum due to the superior $\mathrm{FTO}/$ TNP antireflective layer (Figure 3a), the well-defined perovskite grain morphology and excellent coverage of the perovskite capping layer (Figure 2d) that enhances photon trapping and utilization. The LHE of $\mathrm{TNS}/\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ was slightly higher than that of the TNW/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film. This difference in LHE can be attributed to the different optical transmittance enhancement of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers, as well as the perovskite crystal morphology and geometry.
Figure 3c shows the current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics of the PSCs constructed using TNP–PEG400, TNS–TEG, TNW–IPA nanostructured electrodes and measured under calibrated one sun illumination and in the dark, respectively. The nanostructure-dependent device performance (average with standard deviation) is summarized in Table 1  Devices with TNW–IPA ETLs obtain a PCE of $14.02\pm0.50\%$ , which is slightly higher than the TNS–TEG cells $(13.09\pm0.59\%)$ and higher than the TNP–PEG400 cells $(11.61\pm0.60\%)$ . The 0D TNP–PEG400 devices yielded higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ but lower $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ than the oriented 1D TNW–IPA and 2D TNS–TEG cells. The relatively higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ in TNP–PEG400 and TNS–TEG can be explained by the greater light harvesting in these films, as shown in the LHE curves, Figure S9 in the Supporting Information. Figure 3d shows the incident-photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra. The onset photocurrent at $800~\mathrm{nm}$ is consistent with the optical bandgap of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=1.55\ \mathrm{eV})$ , 17  and the IPCE spectra effectively cover the visible region with relatively flat profiles and high maximum IPCE values above $80\%$ . The peak IPCE value was ${\approx}93\%$ for TNP– PEG400, $91\%$ for TNS–TEG and $89\%$ for TNW–IPA at an incident wavelength of $505~\mathrm{nm}$ . Integration of the overlap of the IPCE spectra with AM 1.5G solar photon flux yielded photocurrent densities of 21.65, 21.20, and $20.55\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for the TNP– PEG400, TNS–TEG, and TNW–IPA devices, respectively, which is in excellent agreement with the experimentally measured $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values. The higher PCEs for PSCs based on TNS–TEG and TNW–IPA are mainly due to the increased $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ which are likely to stem from two effects. One is the better infiltration of the perovskite absorber into the highly porous framework of the TNS or TNW nanostructured films, leading to better confinement of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ within the spaces between adjacent sheets or wires that would reduce the pathlength of the charge carrier before collection. The other is the enhanced electron transport characteristics for TNS or TNW films because of their high-crystallinity as well as direct and short pathway for effective charge transport. As demonstrated by the dark $J{-}V$ curves in Figure 3c, devices prepared with TNW–IPA and TNS–TEG showed a higher dark current onset as compared to devices fabricated with TNP–PEG400, suggesting an effectively inhibited charge recombination in the former cases. 49  The $J{-}V$ characteristics of cells based on TNP, TNS, and TNW thin films were also measured with different scanning directions (reverse scan from forward bias (FB) to short circuit (SC) and forward scan from SC to FB). As seen in Figure S10 in the Supporting Information, the $J{-}V$ hystereses for TNW- and TNS-based PSCs were smaller than the TNP-based PSC, which can be attributed to the improved contact between perovskite and porous TNW or TNS, as well as better electron diffusion in the 1D or 2D oxide nanostructures. 48,52
To better understand the role of electron transfer pathways and charge extraction efficiency at the titania/perovskite interface, the photoluminescence spectra of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ films in various configurations, including Glass/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ Glass/TNP/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $\mathrm{Glass/TNS/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{Glass/TNW/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ , have been collected. As seen in Figure 4a, the photoluminescence emission occurs at similar wavelengths (peaking at $780~\mathrm{nm}$ ) for all the samples. Notably, perovskite in contact with the nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL significantly accelerates the photoluminescence quenching as compared to the perovskite on glass. 29,40  More quenching was observed for $\mathrm{Glass/TNW/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ , indicating charge extraction at the TNW/perovskite interface was more efficient, followed by Glass/TNS $\prime{\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\mathrm{PbI}}_{3}$ then Glass/TNP/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ which is largely driven by the intrinsic morphological and dimensional differences. The differences in the photoluminescence quenching for the nanostructures provide indirect evidence that charge extraction and the electron transfer pathway depends on the ETL nanostructure. A schematic (Figure 4b) illustrates the different electron transfer pathways that would thus affect the charge extraction efficiencies at the interface between different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures and perovskite. It is generally believed that the electron transfer pathways are highly tortuous in TNP films due to the numerous grain boundaries and defects, while the pathways within TNW and TNS films are simpler and more direct due to the 1D or 2D geometry. This improves electron transport and charge extraction, leading to the excellent charge collection within the devices. 53
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is a powerful characterization technique for studying the interfacial charge transfer properties of the photovoltaic devices, and has recently been used to investigate the carrier transport and recombination dynamics in mesostructured PSCs. 54–56  Figure 4c presents the typical Nyquist plots of the PSCs based on the TNP, TNS, and TNS. Two distinct arcs are clearly observed. The high frequency arc could be ascribed to the charge carrier recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ at either the ETL/perovskite or perovskite/HTM interface. 6,55  Here, the high frequency feature was assigned to the charge recombination resistance associated with the TNP/perovskite, TNS/perovskite or TNW/perovskite interface, with extracted $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values tabulated in Table 1  The $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ difference is correlated to the rate of charge carrier extraction and recombination. 26  As expected, the devices that include an ETL with highly oriented crystals (TNS and TNW) have higher charge recombination resistance than TNP. This is in keeping with the expected improved electron extraction due to the crystal structure and suppressed charge recombination at the TNS/ perovskite or TNW/perovskite interfaces, and inhibited the back electron transfer from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ or HTM. The EIS results are consistent with the photoluminescence quenching observed and the gradual decline of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ from $976~\mathrm{mV}$ for TNW to $948~\mathrm{mV}$ for TNS and to $902~\mathrm{mV}$ for TNP. Previous research has shown that highly oriented $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures, such as nanowires or nanosheets, led to improved photovoltaic performance, primarily because of the enhancement in $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ due to improved charge extraction and thus suppressed charge recombination. 11,57  Fast electron extraction in PSCs has been recently reported in a graphene quantum dot modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, significantly improving photovoltaic performance. 58,59 The EIS data reinforces this notion, and an explanation for the performance of the TNS- or TNW-based devices is as follows. First, these oriented nanostructures should have fewer surface trapping sites and defects due to their highly crystalline nature and oriented morphology, which would reduce surface recombination. Second, the high porosity of the 1D and 2D structures would facilitate the infiltration of perovskite, leading to improved contact between the perovskite and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ These factors are beneficial for accelerating charge separation at the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface, and would thus boost the charge collection within the TNS or TNW nanostructured ETL. Here, faster electron transfer from perovskite to the 1D TNW ETL is observed due to two possible factors. One is that the nanowires show a larger surface-to-volume-ratio than nanosheets (owing to their slender and elongated geometry, diameter of $8{-}10~\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $150{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ , Table S1, Supporting Information), which could increase the perovskite/titania interfacial area thus increasing the chance for electron injecting and therefore improving charge separation. Another is the 1D structure provides an effective pathway for electron transfer that minimizes the trapping and thus enhances charge collection. 49  In short, the 1D nanostructures promote electron transport and reduce charge recombination.
In conventional mesostructured PSCs, a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer $(20{-}100\ \mathrm{nm})$ between the TCO substrate and mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold is required. 21  This thin blocking layer is also believed to be a prerequisite for high-performance PSCs because it prevents direct contact between the TCO and perovskite. Here, in spite of the anisotropic and omnidirectional orientation of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanostructures, especially the epitaxial and random coverage of nanowires on FTO glass, the possibility of contact between perovskite, HTM or the back contact electrode with the TCO is reduced. However, insufficient coverage of the TCO by the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films can leave some exposed FTO crystals, which can produce shunts or short-circuits due to direct contact with the perovskite particles. The shunts would cause a drop in $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $F F$ and accordingly PCE. 60  Hence optimization of the TNW-based ETL was undertaken by varying the solvothermal temperature program. First, the solvothermal reaction was set at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to cover the FTO glass with an ultrathin $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer (Figure 5a). Then the temperature was increased to $190~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\textup{h}$ , producing nanowires on the FTO/TBL substrate (Figure 5b). Compared with the FTO/ TNW (Figure 1k), the FTO/TBL/TNW exhibited a flatter surface, which is beneficial for the subsequent deposition of a uniform perovskite layer. A schematic illustrating the fabrication process for the FTO/TBL/TNW ETL electrode can be seen in Figure 5c. The incorporation of the TBL prior to the growth of TNW can be achieved by simply programming the temperature during the solvothermal synthesis.
These optimized TNW-based electrodes with the TBL were used to fabricate PSCs. Figure 6a shows the cross-sectional SEM image of the device incorporating the optimized $\mathrm{TBL}/$ TNW electrode; schematically the structure is shown in Figure 6b. The optimized device contains a $30~\mathrm{nm}$ thick compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin film as the blocking layer, a $150~\mathrm{nm}$ thick TNW/ perovskite nanocomposite active layer, an additional $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ perovskite capping layer, a $150\mathrm{nm}$ spiro-MeOTAD layer, and an $80\mathrm{nm}$ thermally evaporated Au layer as the back contact.
All the photovoltaic parameters improved with incorporation of the TBL. For 10 similar PSCs, the champion device had a PCE of $16.04\%$ (Figure 6c), featuring a much improved $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.60\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and increased $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ $(1007~\mathrm{mV})$ and $F F$ (0.71). The $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ above $1000~\mathrm{{mV}}$ indicates improved charge collection efficiency in the optimized TBL/TNW device when compared to the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the other 1D nanostructured PSCs (normally $\mathrm{<}1000\ \mathrm{mV}$ ). 38,39  As shown in Figure S11 in the Supporting Information, there is still hysteresis present, where a PCE of
$16.04\%$ was recorded in the FB–SC scan direction and a PCE of $15.26\%$ was attained in the opposite (SC–FB) direction. However, the hysteresis is smaller in comparison to many conventional PSCs with mesoporous or planar configurations. 13  The hysteresis may be related to interfacial contact between the charge extraction layer, perovskite absorber, and HTM, as well as the device architecture. 61  Also, the variation in efficiency is very small (PCE decreases from $16.04\%$ to $15.24\%$ when the scan rate increases from 0.1 to $0.5\mathrm{~V~s~}^{-1}$ Figure S12, Supporting Information), indicating that the cell reaches an equilibrium state during the measurement and its output should be very close to the real value. The reproducibility of the PSC fabricated using the optimized TBL/TNW films was tested with a batch of 10 cells, see photovoltaic parameters in Table S4, Supporting Information. An average PCE of $15.41\pm0.46\%$ (a very small standard deviation) was determined, indicating good device reproducibility. The IPCE spectrum with a flat spectral profile and high percentage conversion over a large wavelength range, seen in Figure 6d, indicates the optimized TBL/TNWbased PSCs can harvest solar light efficiently from the near-UV $(\approx350\ \mathrm{nm})$ to the near infrared spectral region $({\approx}780~\mathrm{nm})$ . To the best of our knowledge, this is a record efficiency $(16.04\%)$ for 1D nanostructure-based PSCs.
## 3. Conclusions
A simple and facile solvothermal approach has been developed to fabricate nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films on FTO substrates. Such $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films possess tunable dimensions and nanostructures, and can be applied as an effective charge extraction layer in PSCs. These nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films enhance optical transmittance by reducing reflection, therefore reducing incident light loss and improving the light harvesting efficiency in the PSC devices. Owing to their porous structure and fast electric transfer pathway, 1D nanowire and 2D nanosheet thin films improve infiltration of perovskite and effectively reduce the path to be traveled by the photogenerated charge carriers within the device. This facilitates charge extraction and suppresses charge recombination at the ETL/perovskite interface, thus boosting the charge collection efficiency. Furthermore, an efficiency exceeding $16\%$ has been achieved on PSCs constructed using optimized electrodes consisting of a thin $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ blocking layer and then a nanowire layer. This PSC exhibited a large $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.60\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ a high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1007~\mathrm{{mV}}$ and a high $F F$ of 0.71, as a result of excellent light harvesting and charge collection. This is the highest PCE value reported for nanowire-based PSC devices to date. The present work shows that the solvothermal technique is very promising for the fabrication of titania nanostructures with various dimensions and geometries for PSCs, which is of critical importance to the optimization of light trapping and harvesting, as well as the control of interfacial charge extraction and collection. This approach is expected to incite further development of highly efficient state-of-the-art thin film optoelectronics.
## 4. Experimental Section
$\overline{{T i O_{2}}}$ Thin Film Fabricated via Solvothermal Process: The laserpatterned FTO glass slides (TEC8, Dyesol) were carefully cleaned by ultrasonication with a cleaning agent $5\%$ Hellmanex in water), Milli-Q water, ethanol and then acetone. The $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ films were prepared directly on the FTO glass substrate via a solvent-dependent solvothermal approach without the use of a precoated $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ seed layer. In a typical $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ thin film synthesis, $3\times10^{-3}$ M potassium titanium oxide oxalate dihydrate $(\mathsf{K}_{2}\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{9}\mathsf{T i}{\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , Sigma-Aldrich) was dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ Milli- $Q$ water and stirred for 5 min. Then $75m L$ solvent: ethanol (Chem-Supply), ethylene glycol (Chem-Supply), diethylene glycol (Thermal Fisher), triethylene glycol (Sigma-Aldrich), polyethylene glycol 400 (SigmaAldrich), diethylene glycol monoethyl ether (Sigma-Aldrich), diethylene glycol diethyl ether (Sigma-Aldrich), iso-propanol (Sigma-Aldrich) or benzyl alcohol (Sigma-Aldrich), was added with stirring and maintained for another 15 min. The resulting mixed solution turned transparent very quickly, and was transferred to a $50~\mathrm{\mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave. The patterned and cleaned FTO glass $(2\times1~\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ was immersed into the precursor solution with the FTO side facing down and fixed at a constant angle $(45^{\circ})$ in order to ensure the solvothermal growth was as homogeneous as possible. The solvothermal growth was conducted at $\mathsf{l}90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ . The morphology (nanoparticles, nanosheets or nanowires) of the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ film can be tuned by variation in the solvent. After the autoclave cooled to room temperature, the samples were removed and rinsed with Milli-Q water and ethanol several times, and then annealed at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $\rceil\textrm{h}$ in air to crystallize $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and remove residual organic solvent. It should be noted that the glass side of the substrate was covered with Kapton tape when preparing the diverse $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ nanostructures on the FTO side. After the hydrothermal reaction, the Kapton tape was removed and the glass was washed using absolute ethanol. The synthesized $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ films were soaked in a $20\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~\textmu~}\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ solution at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ and then rinsed with Milli-Q water followed by annealing at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for another $30~\mathrm{min}$ .
Perovskite Solar Cell Fabrication: A 45 wt% $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}{N},{N}.$ - dimethylformamide (DMF, Sigma-Aldrich) solution was prepared with $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (Sigma-Aldrich) and ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ (home-made) at a molar ratio of 1:1. The ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ perovskite films were prepared in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ protected glovebox via a previously reported gas-assisted method. 33  Typically, $25~\upmu\upiota$ $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ solution was spin coated onto $\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ substrates at $6500~\mathsf{r p m}$ for 2 s before a dry Argon gas stream (60 psi) was blown over the film to accelerate the film crystallization for $\rceil0\ s$ . After that the films continued spinning for another $\overline{{18\mathrm{~s~}}}$ . The obtained uniform films were annealed on a hotplate at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\min$ in the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ glovebox.
For the hole transporting material (HTM) solution, $47.6~\mathsf{m g}$ spiroMeOTAD (Taiwan, Lumtec), $7.5~\upmu\up L$ of a stock solution of $500~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulphonyl)imide in acetonitrile, and ${\mathsf{14.4~}}{\mathsf{\upmu L}}$ 4-tert-butylpyridine (Sigma-Aldrich) were dissolved in chlorobenzene $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ , Sigma-Aldrich). The spiro-MeOTAD solution $(25~\upmu\up L)$ was spin coated onto the $\Tilde{\Pi}_{10_{2}/}$ perovskite film at 3000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Finally, the Au layer was deposited by thermal evaporation as the top electrode to a thickness of ${\approx}80~\mathsf{n m}$ .
Characterization: The morphologies of the as-prepared $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ films and perovskite films were characterized by a field emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, Quanta 200F FEI). All the SEM images were obtained without metal sputter coating pretreatment. Crystal phase and crystallinity of the products were identified by using X-ray diffraction (Bruker D8 Advance Diffractometer) with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54]8$ Å). The film transmittance and absorbance spectra were obtained using a UV–vis–NIR spectrophotometer (Lambda 1050, Perkin-Elmer) equipped with a $150~\mathsf{m m}$ integrating sphere. Photocurrent–photovoltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves were measured using a Keithley 2400 source meter under AM $\rceil.5\mathrm{~C~}$ illumination $(700m)\times100^{-2})$ ) provided by an Oriel solar simulator, using a $0.1\ \vee\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ voltage sweep rate. A black mask with an aperture of $0.16~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ was used to define the actual illuminated active area of cells and to avoid the light scattering through the sides. The IPCE spectra were recorded from 380 to $800~\mathsf{n m}$ on a Keithley 2400 source meter under the irradiation of a 300 W xenon lamp fitted with an Oriel Corner stone $260~1/4$ m monochromator. EIS were measured under 0.15 sun illumination at frequencies ranging from $10^{6}–70^{-1}$ Hz on an electrochemical workstation (Zahner, Zennium). The AC signal was $\mathsf{l o m}\mathsf{V}.$ The photoluminescence spectra were collected on a FluoroMax-4
Spectrofluorometer over an emission wavelength from 650 to $850~\mathsf{n m}$ The excitation wavelength was $470\ \mathsf{n m}$ .
